# Generalized Mertens Function Recursion Identity

The **Mertens function** $M(x)=\sum_{n\le x}\mu(n)$ is the running total of the Möbius
tags $\mu(n)\in\{-1,0,1\}$. It oscillates wildly, and its growth is tied to the Riemann
Hypothesis. Below you can explore the identities that let us compute it far faster than
adding one term at a time.

## 1. The core identity

For every integer $x\ge 2$ and every cutoff $u$ with $\lfloor\sqrt{x}\rfloor < u < x$,
$$M(x)=\sum_{k=1}^{\lfloor x/u\rfloor}\mu(k)\,S\!\left(\lfloor x/k\rfloor,u\right).$$
Try it yourself — move the sliders and watch the two sides agree:

{{interactive_demo:0}}

## 2. Why it works: a thousand oscillations collapsing to one

Everything rests on the Fundamental Identity $\sum_{k=1}^{y} M(\lfloor y/k\rfloor)=1$.
The bars below are the individual Mertens terms; watch them cancel to exactly $1$:

{{interactive_demo:1}}

This algorithm precomputes the small values and verifies the collapse:

{{demo:1}}

## 3. The geometry: splitting a hyperbola

The cancellation is a lattice-point count under a hyperbola $k\cdot m\le y$, split
